---
// /more-proof — the proof that is not on the home page: Google's PageSpeed Insights for
// three sites, the E-E-A-T scores of the AI panel, and what none of it shows. The home
// page leads with the panel's SEO and GEO scores. Positioning term
// (tests/positioning.spec.ts): "more proof". Honesty rules (CONTENT_GUIDE.md): every
// figure names its tool and its date; E-E-A-T is explained once, here.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { PROOF, PROOF_MEASURED, PANEL, fmtScore } from '../data/proof';

const rerun = (url: string) =>
  `https://pagespeed.web.dev/analysis?url=${encodeURIComponent(url)}&form_factor=mobile`;
---
<Base
  title="More proof: speed, trust and technical SEO"
  description="More proof for Croftweaver: PageSpeed Insights for three sites built with it, four AI assistants' SEO, GEO and E-E-A-T scores, and what none of it shows."
  image="/images/og/more-proof.jpg"
>
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <h1>More proof: speed, trust and technical SEO</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The home page shows how four AI assistants score SEO and GEO for
      genai-wednesday.de. SEO is how well Google can find and read a site. GEO, short
      for generative engine optimization, is how well AI assistants such as ChatGPT,
      Perplexity or Gemini can read and quote it. Here is the rest: Google's PageSpeed
      Insights for three sites built with Croftweaver, the E-E-A-T scores, and what none
      of it shows. Every figure has a date, and where you can re-run a test, there is a
      link.
    </p>
    <p class="aside">
      All three sites belong to the maintainer of Croftweaver. Read them as worked
      examples, not as a promise for yours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="speed">
    <h2>Speed and technical SEO, measured on <time datetime={PROOF_MEASURED.iso}>{PROOF_MEASURED.label}</time></h2>
    <p>
      The test is Google's PageSpeed Insights, on a simulated phone, for the home page
      of each site: one run each. Scores run from 0 to 100 and vary a little from run
      to run. Its SEO score checks technical basics such as titles, descriptions and
      links a crawler can follow.
    </p>
    <ul class="grid proof-grid" role="list">
      {PROOF.map((p) => (
        <li class="card">
          <h3><a href={p.url}>{p.site}</a></h3>
          <dl class="scores">
            <div><dt>Performance</dt><dd>{p.performance}</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Accessibility</dt><dd>{p.accessibility}</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Best practices</dt><dd>{p.bestPractices}</dd></div>
            <div><dt>SEO</dt><dd>{p.seo}</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <a href={rerun(p.url)} aria-label={`Re-run the test for ${p.site}`}>Re-run the test</a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p>
      These scores cover speed, accessibility, good practice and search basics. They
      say nothing about rankings, which depend on your content and your competition.
      For one of these sites, <a href="/proof">the proof page</a> adds its clicks from
      Google since its launch and a check of which AI assistants name it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="eeat">
    <h2>E-E-A-T: how four AI assistants score trust</h2>
    <p>
      E-E-A-T stands for experience, expertise, authoritativeness and trustworthiness.
      It is what Google's quality raters look for when they judge content. It is not a
      score, and
      <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content">Google says</a>
      it is not a ranking factor by itself. The <a href={PANEL.lab}>Builder Lab</a> asks
      four AI assistants to put a number on it anyway, with the same prompt as for SEO
      and GEO, on <time datetime={PANEL.date.iso}>{PANEL.date.label}</time>. GEO and E-E-A-T have no official scorer, so
      these numbers are AI opinions, not a Google metric.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="SEO, GEO and E-E-A-T scores from four AI assistants for genai-wednesday.de" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Assistant</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">SEO</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">GEO</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">E-E-A-T</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {PANEL.rows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{row.assistant}</th>
              <td class="num">{fmtScore(row.seo)}</td>
              <td class="num">{fmtScore(row.geo)}</td>
              <td class="num">{fmtScore(row.eeat)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
          <tr class="avg">
            <th scope="row">Panel average</th>
            <td class="num">{fmtScore(PANEL.average.seo)}</td>
            <td class="num">{fmtScore(PANEL.average.geo)}</td>
            <td class="num">{fmtScore(PANEL.average.eeat)}</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Scores out of 10, as the Builder Lab shows them (read on <time datetime={PANEL.readOn.iso}>{PANEL.readOn.label}</time>).
      Gemini (Antigravity) answers out of 100 and the Lab shows it normalised to a scale
      of 10; the others answer out of 10 directly. The Lab warns that scores are not
      comparable between assistants, so read the average as a rough guide. The SEO
      column here is an AI opinion of the whole site; the SEO score in the PageSpeed
      cards above is an automatic check of technical basics. The Lab ran the same prompt
      twice, on <time datetime={PANEL.firstRound.iso}>{PANEL.firstRound.label}</time> and on <time datetime={PANEL.date.iso}>{PANEL.date.label}</time>, and keeps both
      rounds with a note for each assistant.
    </p>
    <h3>Try it on your own site</h3>
    <p>This is the Lab's prompt. Paste it into your own AI assistant, then change the address to your own:</p>
    <pre class="message"><code>{PANEL.labPrompt}</code></pre>
    <h3>What the panel says is still missing</h3>
    <p>
      All four assistants name the same next step for genai-wednesday.de: citations and
      links from outside its own domain. Croftweaver builds the signals a page can
      carry. It cannot earn those for you.
    </p>
    <h3>What Croftweaver builds in</h3>
    <p>
      The skills build in structured data that names the organisation and links to
      profiles that are really yours, a named author with a short bio where a page has
      one, cited sources, an imprint, a privacy page and links that work. Tests cover
      part of this: every page must carry structured data a machine can read, and
      links inside the site that are written from the start of the address must work.
      Outside links and profiles are not tested, and no test can tell whether a claim
      is true.
      <a href="/positioning#trust">Positioning first</a> says more.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="limits">
    <h2>What this page does not show</h2>
    <ul class="steps">
      <li><strong>Rankings.</strong> A PageSpeed score and an AI opinion are not a ranking. Rankings depend on your content and your competition.</li>
      <li><strong>That the AI scores repeat.</strong> It is one panel on one site, run twice, and the scores vary by run and by the state of the site.</li>
      <li><strong>Recommendations.</strong> The AI scores are opinions. They do not show that an assistant would recommend the site.</li>
      <li><strong>A comparison.</strong> Nothing here shows what these sites would have scored without Croftweaver.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <a href="/proof">One site in depth</a> has its clicks from Google and a count of
      how often assistants name it. The <a href={PANEL.lab}>Builder Lab</a> keeps the
      full scorecard.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  section.wrap { padding: 2.5rem 1.25rem; max-width: var(--maxw); margin: 0 auto; }
  section.hero { padding-top: 3rem; }
  .hero h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem); line-height: 1.1; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  h2 { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.25; }
  section p, .steps { max-width: 46rem; line-height: 1.7; hyphens: manual; }
  .lead { font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .note { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .steps { padding-left: 1.3rem; }
  .steps li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  pre {
    background: var(--bg-soft); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 1rem 1.2rem; overflow-x: auto; max-width: 46rem;
  }
  pre.message { white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  code { font-size: 0.95rem; }

  /* role="list" in the markup: Safari drops list semantics when list-style is none. */
  .grid {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem; margin: 1.2rem 0; padding: 0; list-style: none;
  }
  .proof-grid .card { padding-left: 1.4rem; margin: 0; color: var(--ink); }
  .scores { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 0.6rem 1rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .scores div { display: flex; flex-direction: column-reverse; }
  .scores dt { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .scores dd { margin: 0; font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.1; color: var(--pass); }

  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 1.2rem 0; max-width: 46rem; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; }
  th, td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); line-height: 1.5; }
  thead th { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); }
  tbody th { font-weight: 400; }
  .num { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  tr.avg th, tr.avg td { font-weight: 700; }
</style>
